#X text 47 41 The [lop~] object is a one pole filter. let's see how to implement it. First we convert the frequency from hertz to radians per sample and use this value as a basis for both the pole and input gain coefficients. But since a maximum value for the coefficient is one \, the maximum frequency input is about 7 Khz., f 61;
#X listbox 694 630 27 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 527 398 And here's also how to implement it with [biquad~]. We just use the input gain coefficient the coefficient for the first pole. The other coefficients are just zeroed out. Unlike the other options \, you cannot control the filter parameter with a signal input., f 55;
#X text 169 602 could be a signal, f 8;
#X text 379 611 could be a signal, f 8;
#X obj 744 85 v a;
#X obj 788 75 expr 1 - $f1;
#X obj 788 103 v b;
#X obj 550 100 fexpr~ a * $x + b * $y;
#X text 524 215 The filter formula then is: y[n] = a*x[n] + b*y[-1];
#X text 537 237 where 'a' is radians per sample and b is "1 - a";
#X obj 652 301 cnv 15 150 50 empty empty empty 5 12 0 14 #fcc4c4 #404040 0;
#X text 699 311 _____________;
#X text 653 317 H(z) =;
#N canvas 861 140 405 314 H(z) 0;
#X text 35 78 We get:;
#X text 34 43 From: y[n] = a * x[n] + b * y[n-1];
#X text 106 108 Y(z) = a * X(z) + b * Y(z)z^-1;
#X text 108 134 a * X(z) = Y(z) - b * Y(z)z^-1;
#X text 109 158 X(z) = (Y(z) - b * Y(z)z^-1) / a;
#X text 74 183 H(z) = a * Y(z) / Y(z) - b * Y(z)z^-1;
#X text 168 228 _____________;
#X text 122 234 H(z) =;
#X text 211 222 a;
#X text 173 245 1 - b * z^-1;
#X restore 749 352 pd H(z);
#X text 658 277 Transfer function:;
#X text 742 305 a;
#X text 704 328 1 - b * z^-1;
#X text 575 352 see how to get there -->;
#X text 669 142 Note you could have a 2nd inlet for signals for controlling the coefficient value, f 31;
#X text 187 257 As seen way back \, radians per sample is just another measure for frequencies and tells you how much the phase (in radians) changes from one sample to the next. Using this as a filter coefficient is not accurate but works approximately well \, specially for lower frequencies. The choice for using it in this Vanilla filter is because it is a cheap and efficient conversion. The [lop2~] object that we'll see next uses a more accurate conversion., f 42;
